CASTING NOTE: The role of Reyansh must be played by a deaf actor to ensure authentic, first-hand representation.

INT. SCHOOL HALLWAY - DAY

Bright lockers line the walls of Oak Creek Elementary. Kids run past. They laugh. They shout. 

REYANSH (10) sits on a wooden bench. He is a South Asian boy with hair swept neatly to one side. He wears a navy tracksuit top with white piping. A purple hearing aid sits behind his left ear. He is a deaf boy. He has profound bilateral hearing loss. 

Reyansh does not look at the running kids. He looks at his hands. 

He holds a folded paper fortune-teller. It is made of purple and white paper. He slips his fingers inside. He flips it open, then closed. Red. Blue. Seven. Eight. 

He wants the Golden Tiger Card. It is a rare, holographic card. It sits in a glass case down the hall. It is the grand prize for today's assembly puzzle.

LEO (10), a non-disabled classmate, walks up. He stands in front of Reyansh. Leo holds a plastic card binder. He taps his foot. He speaks quickly, his chest puffed out. 

REYANSH'S POV: 

Leo's mouth moves fast. No sound. But his shoulders are tight. His eyes dart to the glass case down the hall. His left foot taps three times. He is nervous. He is pretending to be brave. 

BACK TO PLAY:

Reyansh smiles. He does not need to hear the words to know Leo is worried about the puzzle.

Reyansh opens his paper fortune-teller. He points to the blue triangle inside. It says: WATCH CLOSELY.

Leo shrugs. He waves his hand dismissively and walks away toward the gym.

Reyansh puts the paper fortune-teller into his tracksuit pocket. He pats it. He stands up and follows.

INT. SCHOOL GYMNASIUM - DAY

Rows of bleachers are filled with kids. A large stage sits at the front. 

On the stage, there is a wooden podium. Next to it stands a tall, black metal safe with a gold lock. 

PRINCIPAL ARTHUR MILLER (50s) stands at the podium. He is a tall man with thick glasses. He speaks into a microphone. 

Reyansh sits in the third row. Next to him are other students. They are non-disabled kids who look up at the stage, reacting to the loud sound of the microphone.

Reyansh watches Principal Miller's face. The principal's eyebrows are raised. He is excited. 

Principal Miller gestures to the side of the stage. 

A man in a khaki safari outfit walks out. This is DR. CHARLES VANCE (40s). He has a wide smile. He waves. He carries a small wooden box under his arm.

The crowd of kids claps. Reyansh feels the vibrations of the clapping in his chest. He watches Dr. Vance closely.

Dr. Vance steps to the podium. He starts to speak. 

REYANSH'S POV:

Dr. Vance's smile is wide, but his eyes do not crinkle at the corners. His mouth smiles, but his forehead is tense. 

Dr. Vance's hands grip the edges of the podium. His knuckles are white. He looks back at the black metal safe. His left eye twitches.

BACK TO PLAY:

Reyansh leans forward. He taps his chin. Something is wrong with this man. 

Dr. Vance sets the small wooden box on the podium. He reaches into his pocket. He stops. His face changes. 

Dr. Vance looks at Principal Miller. He speaks quickly. He points to the wooden box, then to his pockets.

Principal Miller looks shocked. He steps to the microphone. 

PRINCIPAL MILLER
(on microphone, lips clear)
Students, please remain calm. Dr. Vance reports that the golden key to the prize safe is missing from his podium box. It was here moments ago.

An assistant, MRS. GABLE (40s), runs onto the stage. She looks worried. 

Kids in the bleachers start talking. They look at each other. Some point at their bags. 

Leo turns to Reyansh. He speaks loudly, gesturing wildly.

LEO
Someone stole it! They are going to cancel the contest!

Reyansh does not listen to the panic. He keeps his eyes on Dr. Vance.

Dr. Vance is talking to Mrs. Gable. 

REYANSH'S POV:

Dr. Vance's lips move. 

DR. VANCE (LIP-READ)
It must be a student. Search the bags. Lock the doors.

As Dr. Vance speaks, his left hand slips down. He pats the side of his left high-top safari boot. His eyes dart to the school exit doors. 

BACK TO PLAY:

Reyansh narrows his eyes. Why would a man who lost a key pat his own boot? 

Principal Miller speaks into the microphone again. 

PRINCIPAL MILLER
We must do a quick check. Please stay in your seats. No one leaves the gym.

Two school security guards stand by the doors. 

The kids around Reyansh start to pull open their backpacks. They are confused. 

Reyansh feels a tight knot in his stomach. He hates when things get chaotic. People are shouting, plans are being made, and he is sitting in the middle of a storm he cannot hear. 

He reaches into his pocket. His fingers touch the paper fortune-teller. He pulls it out. 

He looks at the folded corners. He opens it. 

Under the yellow flap, it reads: THE TRUTH IS IN THE DETAILS.

Reyansh looks back at the stage. He ignores the rushing teachers. He ignores the kids dumping their pencil cases. 

He watches Dr. Vance. 

Dr. Vance is now standing near the black safe. He is not looking for the key. He is looking at the gold lock on the safe. He reaches out and touches the dial. 

His face is cold. The fake warm smile is completely gone. His mouth is pressed into a thin, hard line. 

Reyansh looks at the assistant, Mrs. Gable, who is busy searching a box near the stairs. 

Dr. Vance slips a small silver tool from his sleeve. He tries to insert it into the safe lock. 

Reyansh stands up. 

Leo grabs Reyansh's sleeve. 

LEO
Where are you going? We have to stay here!

Reyansh pulls his sleeve away gently. He signs with quick, precise hands. 

REYANSH
(signing)
He has the key. He is lying.

Leo looks confused. He does not understand sign language. 

LEO
What? Just sit down!

Reyansh does not sit down. He walks down the bleacher steps. He moves quickly but quietly. 

He reaches the floor of the gym. He walks toward Principal Miller, who is talking to OFFICER JENKINS (30s) near the stage.

Principal Miller sees Reyansh approaching. He looks stressed. He waves his hand for Reyansh to go back.

PRINCIPAL MILLER
Reyansh, please return to your seat. We are in a lockdown.

Reyansh pulls a small dry-erase board and a black marker from his tracksuit pocket. He always keeps them for quick communication.

He writes quickly in big, clear letters:

THE GUEST IS LYING. THE KEY IS IN HIS LEFT BOOT.

He holds the board up to Principal Miller.

Principal Miller reads the board. He blinks. He looks at Officer Jenkins. 

PRINCIPAL MILLER
Reyansh, Dr. Vance is a famous guest. Why would he hide his own key?

Reyansh writes again:

HE IS NOT DR. VANCE. HE IS TRYING TO OPEN THE SAFE NOW.

Officer Jenkins looks over at the stage. 

Dr. Vance is still standing by the safe. His back is to the crowd. His shoulder is moving in quick, rhythmic jerks as he works on the lock.

Officer Jenkins frowns. She starts to walk toward the stage. Principal Miller and Reyansh follow.

As they get closer, Dr. Vance hears their footsteps on the wooden stage steps. He quickly hides the tool in his sleeve and turns around. The warm, fake smile pops back onto his face. 

DR. VANCE
Ah, Officer! Any luck finding the little thief among the children?

Reyansh watches the man's face. The man's upper lip is sweating. His eyes are wide with alarm.

Officer Jenkins steps onto the stage. 

OFFICER JENKINS
Dr. Vance, we need to clear up a quick detail. Would you mind showing us your left boot?

Dr. Vance's smile freezes. His left cheek twitches twice. 

DR. VANCE
My boot? Why on earth would you ask that? This is ridiculous. I am the victim here!

He takes a step back. His eyes dart to the side exit.

Reyansh points directly at the man's left boot. He looks at Officer Jenkins and nods firmly. 

OFFICER JENKINS
Just a quick check, sir. For peace of mind.

Dr. Vance suddenly bolts. He runs toward the side exit door.

Officer Jenkins is fast. She blocks the door. She grabs his arm and spins him around. 

OFFICER JENKINS
Stop right there!

Principal Miller gasps. The entire gym goes silent. Hundreds of kids watch from the bleachers.

Officer Jenkins taps the man's left boot. A heavy golden key slides out of the top fold of the leather and clatters onto the wooden stage floor.

It is the golden key.

Officer Jenkins picks it up. She looks at the man.

OFFICER JENKINS
You have some explaining to do. Let's start with your real name.

She leads the fake Dr. Vance away through the side door. 

Principal Miller stands in shock. He looks at the golden key in his hand. He looks at Reyansh.

PRINCIPAL MILLER
How did you know? Nobody else saw a thing.

Reyansh takes his dry-erase board. He writes:

PEOPLE TALK WITH THEIR MOUTHS. THEY TELL THE TRUTH WITH THEIR FACES.

Principal Miller smiles. He nods with deep respect.

Just then, the double doors at the back of the gym open. 

A second man in a safari outfit enters. He looks out of breath. He has a badge around his neck. He is accompanied by another school guard.

REAL DR. VANCE
I am so sorry I am late! Someone tied up my assistant and stole my truck this morning! I had to take a taxi!

The kids in the bleachers cheer. 

Principal Miller walks to the podium. He speaks into the microphone.

PRINCIPAL MILLER
Students, the lockdown is over. The imposter has been caught thanks to some incredible detective work.

Principal Miller gestures to Reyansh.

PRINCIPAL MILLER
And now, for the puzzle contest. Today's puzzle was to find the hidden truth. Only one student solved it.

Principal Miller reaches into the glass case on the stage. He pulls out the holographic Golden Tiger Card. It shimmers in the gym lights, flashing gold and green. 

PRINCIPAL MILLER
Reyansh, come on up.

Reyansh walks to the center of the stage. 

He receives the Golden Tiger Card. He holds it up. 

The gym erupts in applause. Reyansh feels the wonderful, heavy vibration of their cheers shaking the floorboards under his feet. 

He looks at the front row. Leo is clapping wildly, looking at Reyansh with wide, amazed eyes. 

Reyansh reaches into his pocket. He pulls out his purple and white paper fortune-teller. He holds it in one hand, and his Golden Tiger Card in the other. 

He is a deaf boy. He did not need to hear a single word to find the truth.

He smiles, his eyes crinkling at the corners. This time, the smile is completely real.

EXT. SCHOOL YARD - DAY

School is dismissed. Kids walk toward the buses. 

Reyansh stands by the bike rack. He looks at his Golden Tiger Card. It is perfect. 

Leo walks up. He does not boast. He looks at the card, then at Reyansh. 

Leo raises his hands. He clumsily signs two words he must have looked up on his phone.

LEO
(signing clumsily)
Thank you. Cool.

Reyansh's face lights up. He signs back with quick, precise, and beautiful movements.

REYANSH
(signing)
You are welcome. Want to see my fortune-teller?

Leo nods. 

Reyansh pulls out the paper fortune-teller. He open and closes it. Red. Blue. Seven. Eight. 

They laugh together in the warm afternoon sun. 

THE END